        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Core Adult Sizes Shortcut (Sizes 2, 3, 4)",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If you only need to rapidly recall the 3 main adult sizes used in clinical practice:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "\"Green Yellow Red\" $\\rightarrow$ Traffic Light Order"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Green (Size 2 / 80mm): Small Adult"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Yellow (Size 3 / 90mm): Medium Adult"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Red (Size 4 / 100mm): Large Adult"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Memory Trick: Starts at 80 mm for Green and goes up by 10 mm per step"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "How to Measure & Select the Correct Size",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To ensure correct fit and prevent airway obstruction or soft tissue trauma:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "\"Corner to Angle\""
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measurement: Measure from the Corner of the mouth to the Angle of the mandible (or from the center of the incisors to the angle of the jaw)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Too Large: Pushes the epiglottis posteriorly, causing complete airway obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Too Small: Pushes the tongue back into the pharynx, worsening obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The usual bedside method is to hold the OPA against the side of the face and compare its length with the patient's anatomy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common OSCE method: Measure from the corner of the mouth to the angle of the mandible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some teaching protocols use the corner of the mouth to the tragus of the ear. Follow the sizing method taught in your local clinical protocol and the device manufacturer's guidance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Too short: May fail to displace the tongue adequately and may push it backward, worsening obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Too long: May traumatise the airway or stimulate the laryngeal area, potentially causing complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not select a size solely by the patient's age or the colour of the device."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "How to insert an OPA — practical OSCE checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Demonstrate on a manikin unless the clinical situation and supervision permit otherwise."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Insertion checklist",
                    "0/8 completed"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Perform hand hygiene, use appropriate PPE, and explain the procedure if the patient is responsive enough to understand."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess consciousness, breathing, airway patency, and the gag reflex; confirm that an OPA is appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Position the patient and open the airway using the appropriate manoeuvre."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the mouth and remove visible loose material; suction secretions if indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select the correct OPA size using anatomical landmarks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert carefully using the technique appropriate for the patient's age."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the flange rests at the lips and that the airway remains patent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess chest movement, airflow, oxygenation, and ventilation effectiveness."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_12",
              "group_title": "Insertion technique in adults",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Position the head appropriately and open the mouth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If necessary, use a tongue-depressor technique to prevent the tongue from being pushed backward."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert the OPA under direct vision, following the curve of the oral cavity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advance gently until the flange rests at the lips."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess airway patency and ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some adult protocols teach insertion with the airway initially rotated 180 degrees, then rotated into position as it advances. This technique should not be used routinely in infants and young children because it may injure the palate or soft tissues. A tongue-depressor/direct-vision approach is preferable in children."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Never force the device against resistance. If gagging, vomiting, or deterioration occurs, remove it and reassess the airway."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_13",
              "group_title": "Main contraindication",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An intact gag reflex is the classic contraindication to OPA insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Consciousness or partial consciousness."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Active gagging or vomiting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Significant oral trauma or an inability to open the mouth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect sizing or inability to insert safely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An OPA is not a treatment for a foreign-body obstruction and does not protect the airway from aspiration."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Gagging and vomiting",
                      "Stimulation of the pharynx in a patient with an intact gag reflex"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "Vomit or secretions may enter the airway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Airway obstruction",
                      "Incorrect size or displacement of the tongue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Oral or pharyngeal trauma",
                      "Forceful or inappropriate insertion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Dental injury",
                      "Trauma during insertion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Laryngospasm",
                      "Possible response to airway stimulation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hypoxia",
                      "Persistent obstruction, ineffective ventilation, or delayed correction of the problem"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Pediatric and neonatal considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Choose the size carefully; the smallest airway is not automatically the correct one."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Children and infants have smaller airways and more delicate oral tissues."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid rotating the OPA blindly through 180 degrees in infants and young children."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use direct visualisation or a tongue depressor when appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An OPA should be used only when the patient's level of consciousness and gag reflex make it appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In neonates, airway management should follow neonatal resuscitation protocols and the training of the clinician."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "OPA versus NPA",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This comparison is frequently tested in OSCEs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Oropharyngeal airway (OPA)",
                    "Nasopharyngeal airway (NPA)"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Route",
                      "Mouth",
                      "Nose"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Common use",
                      "Unconscious patient without an intact gag reflex",
                      "Selected patients who need an airway adjunct but cannot tolerate an OPA"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gag reflex",
                      "Usually must be absent",
                      "May be tolerated better when the gag reflex is present"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main precautions",
                      "Intact gag reflex, oral trauma, poor sizing",
                      "Suspected basal skull fracture, significant nasal trauma, bleeding risk"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Insertion",
                      "Through the mouth",
                      "Through the nostril"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration protection",
                      "No",
                      "No"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Neither device provides definitive airway protection."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Memory aid: OPA",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "O"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Oral route: Insert through the mouth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "P"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Proper size and position: Measure anatomically and insert gently."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Absent gag reflex: The key requirement for routine OPA use."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_17",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE examiner questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a Guedel airway?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An oropharyngeal airway adjunct used to maintain upper-airway patency in an appropriately unconscious patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. How do you size it?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually from the corner of the mouth to the angle of the mandible, following the local sizing protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the main contraindication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An intact gag reflex."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why can an OPA worsen airway obstruction?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An incorrectly sized or positioned OPA can push the tongue backward or fail to maintain the airway adequately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Does an OPA prevent aspiration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It does not provide reliable protection against aspiration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What should you do if the patient starts gagging?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stop, remove the OPA, reassess the airway and level of consciousness, and consider an alternative approach."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What is the difference between an OPA and an endotracheal tube?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An OPA is a basic airway adjunct that helps maintain patency. An endotracheal tube is an advanced airway device placed into the trachea by a trained clinician and can facilitate controlled ventilation and airway protection, although aspiration protection is not absolute."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_subsection_18",
          "subsection_title": "Mini OSCE scenario",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oropharyngeal_airway_guedel_airway_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scenario: An unconscious adult is snoring and has no effective gag reflex. You are asked to select and insert an airway adjunct."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Expected answer:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the airway, breathing, and overall clinical condition."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open the airway and assess whether an OPA is appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select the correct size using anatomical landmarks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert gently using an appropriate technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the flange remains outside the mouth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess airway patency and ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Continue appropriate oxygenation, ventilation, and resuscitation as indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examiner's key point: An OPA is appropriate for an unconscious patient without an intact gag reflex, but correct sizing and reassessment are essential."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}